---
/**
 * Gives the homepage crawlable subject matter and internal links into the
 * collections. The parallax wall renders to a WebGL canvas, so without this the
 * page carries almost no indexable text.
 */
---

<section class="container-custom py-20">
	<div class="prose mx-auto max-w-2xl text-gray-700">
		<h2 class="reveal text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold">Photography in Barcelona</h2>

		<p class="reveal">
			I photograph <a href="/collections/weddings/">weddings</a>, <a href="/collections/events/"
				>events</a
			> and <a href="/collections/activism/">documentary work</a> in Barcelona and wherever else the
			work takes me. My approach centers on raw emotion, genuine reaction, natural movement and minimal
			editing.
		</p>

		<p class="reveal">
			That covers ceremonies and celebrations, corporate evenings and networking nights, sport,
			concerts and <a href="/collections/events/nightclub/">nightlife</a>, and long-form
			journalistic projects — Pride in Barcelona, an immigration-rights protest by Houston's Mexican
			community, and a memorial held by Houston's Ukrainian community on the anniversary of the war.
		</p>

		<p class="reveal">
			I work in English and Spanish, and much of my client work in Barcelona is with visitors and
			people who have recently moved here. I travel for commissions — the documentary and event work
			below was photographed in Barcelona, on the Costa Brava, and in Houston, Texas.
		</p>

		<p class="reveal">
			There is also work I am actively building and shooting at reduced rates or TFP, including
			creative and conceptual shoots. <a href="/book/">The details are on the booking page.</a>
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	/**
	 * Scroll-driven sequential reveal: each block fades up as it enters view,
	 * then sits as an ordinary paragraph column.
	 *
	 * This is the only crawlable text on the homepage, so it must never be
	 * possible for it to end up permanently invisible. Three guards:
	 *
	 * 1. No JavaScript. A scroll listener that fails to attach would leave the
	 *    text at opacity 0 forever; a CSS animation cannot fail that way.
	 * 2. The hidden start state lives INSIDE @supports, so a browser without
	 *    animation-timeline never hides what it has no way to reveal. The base
	 *    state of every .reveal is fully visible.
	 * 3. prefers-reduced-motion opts out completely — vestibular disorders make
	 *    movement-on-scroll genuinely unpleasant, not just a taste question.
	 *
	 * The range completes early (by 28% of the way through the element's pass
	 * across the viewport) so text is solid well before it reaches reading
	 * position, rather than still fading while someone is trying to read it.
	 */
	@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
		@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
			.reveal {
				animation: reveal-in linear both;
				animation-timeline: view();
				animation-range: entry 10% cover 28%;
			}
		}
	}

	@keyframes reveal-in {
		from {
			opacity: 0;
			transform: translateY(14px);
		}
		to {
			opacity: 1;
			transform: none;
		}
	}
</style>
